A||N||5||sanderson||Brake Line Replacement||||5||
Z||000000||sanderson||09-24-2001||08:37 PM||||I have been trying to replace a corroded brake line on my 1984.  It is the main line from the master cylinder to the rear of the car.  This is a metric system and I'm not sure if the flares are standard metric or metric "bubble flares".  Tubing look like 6MM.  I tried to use 1/4" U.S. tubing with the old metric male ends.  I did standard U.S. double flare but this leaks.  <P>One can buy short pigtails of 3/16" tubing with metric ends and then use couplings to fit up for longer runs.  All the chain stores have them for 3/16" but nobody has them or even thinks they can be ordered for 1/4". <P>I have read some info on net that one can make a metric "bubble flare" by just doing the first step of a U.S. double flare.  Is this true and are the Fiero fittings in fact metric "bubble flares"?  If these are standard metric flares, is there a tool available?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DreXteR||09-24-2001||10:25 PM||||1st welcome to the forum. As far I know about lines the fiero is an enigma. I have a problem with lines and end buying a ss ones. Not cheap but is my life that will be in danger.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||09-25-2001||12:39 AM||||I replaced all the brakelines recently on my 84. I just got a roll of regular brake line, I reused my ends (just cleaned them up and made sure there were no sharp spots)(old pipe can be drilled out safely if it is rusted off)<P>Yes, it is ISO flared (aka bubble or double flared)   Check around at a few auto repair stores,  some have loan-a-tool programs that can lend you the iso flare tool to do it..   or if you can't locate one, pre-fit the pipe on the car, then take it off again and take it to a auto store and get them to flare it for you (little or no charge prob.)<P>If you do rent a tool, practice on a short piece until you have the flaring looking like the stock (took me a few tries   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)   also if you do pre-fit it on the car, tape the ends with black tape so dirt dont go inside the lines while being put into place..<P>Good luck.. any problems, feel free to pm me.<P><BR>PS.. welcome to the best fiero board out there!<P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||09-25-2001||02:32 AM||||Your double flared ends leak? <P>Shouldn't  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <P>Are you sure they were tightened enough?  You do have to put a fair ammount of torque on them.<P>1/4" line double-flared should work fine. (it can be tricky to get the flare correct.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||isthiswhereiputausername?||09-25-2001||01:20 PM||||also make sure the flare end is straight.   they dont need to be perfectly "flared" just enough to grab the end.. but it needs to be straight & flat so it will grab a good seal..  I believe torque on the lines is 22-23 pounds.. which is roughly a tight seal without overdoing it.. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||BurlyGoat||09-26-2001||12:49 PM||||I  just replaced all my brake lines on a 86 se and I used a 1/4 inch line and used the old ends..No Leaks||||reg||1
